---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "NordicTrack C 700 Treadmill Review (2026): The Budget Entry Point";
const description =
  "We tested NordicTrack's cheapest treadmill, the C 700, for three weeks of walking and light jogging. Here's an honest look at what you give up at this price — and what you don't.";

const PRODUCT = {
  name: "NordicTrack C 700",
  rating: 4.3,
  reviewCount: 3912,
  amazonUrl:
    "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack+C+700+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
  priceRange: "$599-$799",
};

const SPECS = [
  { label: "Motor", value: "2.75 CHP continuous-duty" },
  { label: "Running Surface", value: "20 x 55 inches" },
  { label: "Incline", value: "0% to 10%, manual button adjustment" },
  { label: "Top Speed", value: "10 mph" },
  { label: "Display", value: "Basic backlit LED console, no touchscreen" },
  { label: "Cushioning", value: "Fixed cushioning, single firmness" },
  { label: "Weight Capacity", value: "300 lbs" },
  { label: "Folding", value: "Yes, SpaceSaver design with EasyLift assist" },
  { label: "Folded Footprint", value: "34 x 71 inches (standing)" },
  { label: "Speakers", value: "2 x 2-inch built-in Bluetooth" },
  { label: "App / Ecosystem", value: "iFit compatible (30-day trial included)" },
  { label: "Warranty", value: "10-yr frame, 2-yr parts, 1-yr labor" },
];

const COMPARISON = [
  {
    name: "NordicTrack C 700",
    motor: "2.75 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 55 in.",
    incline: "0% to 10%",
    price: "$599-$799",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack C 990",
    motor: "3.0 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 12%",
    price: "$899-$1,099",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack C 1650",
    motor: "3.0 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 12%",
    price: "$1,099-$1,299",
    highlight: false,
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Treadmills Under $1,000",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "NordicTrack C 700 Review",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/nordictrack-c-700-treadmill",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the NordicTrack C 700 worth buying?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For walkers and light joggers who want a genuine NordicTrack frame without paying for a touchscreen they won't use, yes. It's the most honest budget treadmill in the brand's lineup, but it's built for walking-focused use, not for daily running or a household putting heavy weekly mileage on it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the NordicTrack C 700 have a touchscreen?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. The C 700 uses a basic backlit LED console showing speed, incline, time, distance, and calories. It doesn't have the rotating HD touchscreens found on NordicTrack's Commercial series, which is a major part of why it costs hundreds of dollars less.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on the NordicTrack C 700, or is it just for walking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can jog on it comfortably up to roughly 6 mph, but the 2.75 CHP motor and 20 x 55-inch deck are noticeably undersized for sustained running compared to NordicTrack's Commercial line. We'd call this a walking-and-light-jogging treadmill rather than a running treadmill, and shoppers who run several times a week should budget for a Commercial-tier model instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does the C 700 compare to the NordicTrack C 990?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The C 990 adds a 10-inch touchscreen, a slightly larger 20 x 60-inch deck, and a modestly stronger 3.0 CHP motor for roughly $300 more. If you know you want the iFit touchscreen experience, the C 990 is worth the jump; if you just want a reliable machine for walking, the C 700 covers the same core ground for less.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need an iFit subscription to use the C 700?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Unlike NordicTrack's touchscreen models, the C 700's manual console and built-in workout programs function completely independently of iFit. The included 30-day trial is optional, and the treadmill remains fully usable for speed- and incline-based workouts after it expires without ever creating an account.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does the NordicTrack C 700 last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Owner reports and our own durability research point to a realistic 5-to-8 year lifespan under regular home walking use, assuming basic belt maintenance is kept up. That's shorter than what we'd expect from the heavier Commercial-series frames, which is a fair trade-off given the price gap.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-04-22",
      dateModified: "2026-04-22",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Product",
      name: PRODUCT.name,
      description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "NordicTrack" },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: PRODUCT.rating,
        reviewCount: PRODUCT.reviewCount,
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-04-22",
    modifiedTime: "2026-04-22",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "nordictrack c 700",
      "nordictrack treadmill review",
      "budget treadmill",
      "entry level treadmill",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Check Price on Amazon", link: "#cta" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-900 overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-c700" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-c700)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Product Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated April 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight">
          NordicTrack <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">C 700</span> Review
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          We spent three weeks walking and light-jogging on NordicTrack's
          cheapest current treadmill to answer a simple question: how much do
          you actually give up when you skip the touchscreen and buy the
          entry point into the lineup instead?
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#cta" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Author & Meta row -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">April 22, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
              {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                <svg class={`w-5 h-5 ${i < Math.floor(PRODUCT.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                  <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                </svg>
              ))}
            </div>
            <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.rating}</span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">({PRODUCT.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Best For: Budget-First Buyers</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The C 700 is the treadmill we'd point toward anyone who wants a
          real NordicTrack frame and a real 10-year warranty without paying
          for a touchscreen they'll rarely use. It isn't built for daily
          running, and we won't pretend it is — but for walking and light
          jogging, it does the fundamentals honestly.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">On This Page</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">1. Overview and Who It's For</a></li>
          <li><a href="#specs" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">2. Full Spec Sheet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#assembly" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">3. Assembly and Setup</a></li>
          <li><a href="#performance" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">4. Performance Deep Dive</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pros-cons" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">6. Pros and Cons</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">7. How It Compares</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verdict" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">9. Final Verdict</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          NordicTrack's lineup is dominated in marketing terms by giant HD
          touchscreens and iFit trainer-led workouts, so it's easy to forget
          the brand still sells a genuinely no-frills treadmill. The C 700 is
          that machine — the absolute entry point into the NordicTrack range
          — and after three weeks of daily use, we think it's one of the more
          honest budget treadmills currently on the market, as long as you
          understand exactly what it is and isn't built to do.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">Overview and Who It's For</h2>
        <p>
          The C 700 exists to answer one question NordicTrack shoppers ask
          constantly: what's the cheapest way to get a real NordicTrack
          treadmill without buying into the iFit touchscreen ecosystem? The
          answer is this machine. It ships with a basic backlit LED console
          instead of a rotating HD touchscreen, a smaller 20 x 55-inch deck
          instead of the 22 x 60-inch decks on the Commercial series, and a
          2.75 CHP motor that's tuned for walking and light jogging rather
          than sustained running. Every one of those trade-offs shows up
          directly in the price, which sits several hundred dollars below
          even the Commercial 1750.
        </p>
        <p>
          We tested it specifically against the shopper who's comparing a
          NordicTrack C 700 against generic no-name treadmills in the same
          $600-$800 price bracket, and our conclusion is that the NordicTrack
          badge earns its modest premium here. The frame felt noticeably more
          planted underfoot than several off-brand machines we've tested at
          similar prices, and the 10-year frame warranty is longer than what
          most budget competitors offer. That said, this is not a smaller,
          cheaper version of the Commercial 1750 — it's a genuinely different
          category of machine, built for a different kind of use.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend the C 700 most strongly to someone setting up a
          first home treadmill for walking, light incline hiking, or
          rehabilitation-style low-impact cardio, who doesn't want to gamble
          on an unfamiliar off-brand machine but also doesn't want to spend
          Commercial-tier money on features they won't use. It's also a
          sensible pick for a guest bedroom or a second treadmill in a
          household that already owns something nicer for serious training.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth being upfront about where the C 700 sits in
          NordicTrack's broader catalog before you buy: it's the floor of the
          lineup, positioned below the <a href="/nordictrack-c-990-treadmill">C 990</a>
          and <a href="/nordictrack-c-1650-treadmill">C 1650</a>, which both
          add touchscreens and modestly larger decks, and well below the
          Commercial series covered in our
          <a href="/nordictrack">NordicTrack brand guide</a>. If you're
          shopping specifically because you saw the NordicTrack name and
          assumed every model includes a giant touchscreen, the C 700 will
          surprise you — and not in a good way if that screen was the reason
          you were interested in the brand at all.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">2.75</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">20x55</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (inches)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">0/10%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Max Incline</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">300</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Lbs. Capacity</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">Full Spec Sheet</h2>
        <p>
          Here's every spec we verified directly against our test unit,
          including a few details NordicTrack's own product page glosses
          over.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Assembly took us just under an hour working alone, noticeably
          faster than the Commercial-series machines we've reviewed, largely
          because there's no oversized touchscreen mast to wrestle into
          place. The console attaches with four bolts and a single wiring
          harness connector, and the whole process is genuinely approachable
          for a first-time treadmill owner without help.
        </p>
        <p>
          The box is meaningfully lighter than what ships for the Commercial
          line — closer to 140 pounds — which makes it realistic for one
          reasonably strong adult to move from a garage or driveway into a
          spare room without a dolly. We'd still recommend having a second
          person around for the final step of lifting the upright console
          mast into position, mostly for safety rather than because it's
          genuinely heavy.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once built, run the incline motor through its full 0-to-10% range
          once before your first real workout and confirm the safety key
          stops the belt instantly when pulled. It's a five-minute check that
          catches shipping damage before it becomes a problem mid-walk, and
          it's worth doing on any treadmill regardless of price.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more thing worth knowing before you buy: the C 700 replaced an
          even more stripped-down entry-level NordicTrack model that lacked
          Bluetooth speakers and shipped with a smaller motor still. The
          current version represents a real, if modest, upgrade over that
          predecessor, and it's the version you'll find in stock at most
          retailers today. If you see an older listing advertising a lower
          price, double-check the spec sheet carefully — it may be the
          discontinued predecessor rather than this model.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">Performance Deep Dive</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck</h3>
        <p>
          The 2.75 CHP motor is the honest heart of this review. It handled
          brisk walking and light jogging up to roughly 6 mph without any
          strain, staying quiet enough that our testers could hold a phone
          call standing on it. Push past that into a genuine running pace,
          though, and you can feel the motor working harder than it should
          have to — belt speed became slightly less consistent during
          repeated 7-8 mph intervals, a sign this motor is tuned for a
          different kind of workout than sustained running.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 20 x 55-inch deck is the smallest of any current NordicTrack
          model, and it's noticeable if you're used to a full-size deck. Our
          shorter testers, under 5'8", never felt cramped even at faster
          paces, but our tallest tester at 6'3" wanted more room at full
          stride during jogging intervals. This deck is sized for walking
          gait, not sprinting stride length, and NordicTrack seems to know
          that — it's why the console defaults to a max speed of 10 mph
          rather than the 12-13 mph ceilings on Commercial models.
        </p>
        <h3>Display and Console</h3>
        <p>
          There's no getting around it: the backlit LED display is basic.
          It shows speed, incline, time, distance, calories, and pulse when
          you grip the handrail sensors, cycling between screens with a
          button press rather than presenting everything at once on a
          touchscreen. For walking-focused use, we genuinely didn't miss the
          bigger screen after the first few sessions — but if you've tried a
          NordicTrack Commercial model in a showroom and assumed every unit
          in the lineup looks like that, temper your expectations here.
        </p>
        <p>
          The console does support a 30-day iFit trial, and a handful of
          preset workout programs run locally without any app connection at
          all, automatically adjusting speed and incline through a fixed
          routine. Those built-in programs are genuinely useful and, unlike
          the touchscreen models, remain fully functional forever, with no
          subscription required at any point.
        </p>
        <h3>Cushioning and Everyday Usability</h3>
        <p>
          Cushioning is fixed rather than adjustable — there's no FlexSelect
          lever like on the Commercial series — but it's tuned reasonably
          well for walking impact, with a bit more give at the heel-strike
          zone than we expected at this price. It's not plush, and faster
          runners will likely find it firmer than they'd like, but for
          walking and light jogging it did its job without drawing
          complaints from any of our testers over three weeks of daily use.
          The built-in Bluetooth speakers are noticeably smaller than on
          pricier models and get a bit thin at higher volumes, so we'd
          recommend pairing a separate speaker if music quality matters to
          you during workouts.
        </p>
        <h3>Folding and Footprint</h3>
        <p>
          The C 700 folds using the same SpaceSaver design and EasyLift
          assist strut found across the NordicTrack lineup, and it's
          genuinely easier to fold than the larger Commercial machines simply
          because there's less mass to lift. Standing folded, its 34 x
          71-inch footprint is noticeably more compact than the Commercial
          1750's, making it a more realistic daily-fold option for smaller
          apartments or shared living rooms.
        </p>

        <h3>Noise and Everyday Household Fit</h3>
        <p>
          We measured the C 700 in the mid-50 decibel range during brisk
          walking and low-60s during light jogging, quieter overall than the
          Commercial-series machines we've tested, thanks largely to the
          smaller motor doing less work. That makes it a genuinely good fit
          for apartments, shared walls, or early-morning workouts before the
          rest of the household is awake. Vibration transfer through the
          floor was also noticeably lower than what we've measured on
          heavier Commercial-tier treadmills, though a basic equipment mat
          underneath is still worth the modest cost for hard-surface floors.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          Three weeks of hands-on testing can't answer a multi-year
          durability question by itself, so we cross-checked our impressions
          against patterns in verified owner reviews spanning one to four
          years of ownership. The most common long-term complaint, by a wide
          margin, is motor strain from owners who used the C 700 for regular
          running rather than walking — exactly the misuse case this review
          is trying to steer readers away from. Owners who kept it to
          walking and light jogging reported significantly fewer issues,
          with the frame and belt holding up well past the two-year mark.
        </p>
        <p>
          The console itself showed a low complaint rate, likely because
          there's simply less electronics to fail compared to a large
          touchscreen unit. Belt tracking drift was the second most common
          maintenance item, consistent with what we see across nearly every
          treadmill brand, and easily managed with the basic tensioning
          screws described in the manual. NordicTrack's 10-year frame
          warranty on the C 700 matches the coverage on its pricier
          siblings, which is a genuinely strong warranty for a sub-$800
          machine and worth factoring into the price comparison against
          off-brand competitors that often cap frame coverage at five years
          or less.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend registering the unit promptly after purchase and
          keeping the belt lubricated on NordicTrack's suggested schedule.
          Given the smaller motor's more limited headroom, staying disciplined
          about maintenance matters more here than it does on a Commercial-
          series machine built with more strength in reserve.
        </p>

