      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The step up to the <a href="/nordictrack-c-990-treadmill">C 990</a>
          buys you a 10-inch touchscreen, a slightly larger 20 x 60-inch
          deck, and a modestly stronger 3.0 CHP motor for roughly $300 more —
          worth it if you know you want the iFit touchscreen experience even
          in a smaller package. The <a href="/nordictrack-c-1650-treadmill">C 1650</a>
          pushes further still, with a larger 14-inch touchscreen and the
          same 3.0 CHP motor and 20 x 60-inch deck as the C 990, aimed at
          shoppers who want the biggest screen available before crossing into
          Commercial-tier pricing entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's also worth putting the C 700 next to NordicTrack's much
          pricier Commercial line, covered in depth in our
          <a href="/commercial-treadmills">commercial treadmill roundup</a>.
          The gap isn't just a bigger screen: Commercial-series machines use
          meaningfully larger 22 x 60-inch decks, stronger 3.75-to-4.5 CHP
          motors, and adjustable FlexSelect cushioning that the C 700 simply
          doesn't offer. If your budget can stretch there and you run
          regularly, that gap is worth paying for. If you're mostly walking,
          the C 700 gets you real NordicTrack build quality without paying
          for headroom you won't use.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
