          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack C 700 worth buying?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              For walkers and light joggers who want a genuine NordicTrack
              frame without paying for a touchscreen they won't use, yes.
              It's the most honest budget treadmill in the brand's lineup,
              but it's built for walking-focused use, not for daily running
              or a household putting heavy weekly mileage on it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the NordicTrack C 700 have a touchscreen?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. The C 700 uses a basic backlit LED console showing speed,
              incline, time, distance, and calories. It doesn't have the
              rotating HD touchscreens found on NordicTrack's Commercial
              series, which is a major part of why it costs hundreds of
              dollars less.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on the NordicTrack C 700, or is it just for walking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              You can jog on it comfortably up to roughly 6 mph, but the 2.75
              CHP motor and 20 x 55-inch deck are noticeably undersized for
              sustained running compared to NordicTrack's Commercial line.
              We'd call this a walking-and-light-jogging treadmill rather
              than a running treadmill, and shoppers who run several times a
              week should budget for a Commercial-tier model instead.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the C 700 compare to the NordicTrack C 990?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The C 990 adds a 10-inch touchscreen, a slightly larger 20 x
              60-inch deck, and a modestly stronger 3.0 CHP motor for
              roughly $300 more. If you know you want the iFit touchscreen
              experience, the C 990 is worth the jump; if you just want a
              reliable machine for walking, the C 700 covers the same core
              ground for less.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an iFit subscription to use the C 700?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. Unlike NordicTrack's touchscreen models, the C 700's manual
              console and built-in workout programs function completely
              independently of iFit. The included 30-day trial is optional,
              and the treadmill remains fully usable for speed- and
              incline-based workouts after it expires without ever creating
              an account.
            </p>
